tests:
  unit:
    count: 49
    covers: "src/api/curl.rs (46, incl. 4 proptests) in five modules. tokenizer (11): whitespace splitting and quoted joins; the single-quote idiom 'it'\\''s'; the four double-quote escapes with $HOME kept literal because the command is data, not a script; bare backslash; backslash-newline continuation inside and outside quotes including CRLF from a Windows paste; adjacent quoted segments forming one word; every ANSI-C escape incl. octal, \\U, an unknown escape kept literal as bash keeps it, and a raw \\xff byte surviving because the tokenizer works on bytes; an empty quoted word is a word; every unterminated form refused; a trailing backslash kept. flags (16): both URL spellings; the three method rules (GET, POST when a body flag is present, -X wins) with the method kept as typed; -d '' as a POST with an Empty body; header order and value trim with curl's three header forms (Name: v, Name; for empty, Name: for send-nothing); the five data spellings joined with &; --data-raw @ literal versus the four spellings that read a file, each refused; cookies joined into one header and a jar file refused; basic auth expanded with the exact base64 and the no-password refusal; --compressed's exact value; expansions keeping their place among typed headers with Cookie last; NO runtime default invented (asserted as an empty header list on a -d request); -- ending the flags; short clusters and attached values (-sSkL -XPOST -HX-A:1 -d'a=1'); all 55 ignored spellings in one command yielding a flow identical to the plain one; an ignored flag's value never taken as the URL. refusals (9): one per row of the plan's refusal table, each asserting both the variant and its Display text - empty, not-curl (with curl, /usr/bin/curl, ./curl and curl.exe accepted), ten unsupported flags by name, eight value-taking flags missing their value plus -X '', no URL and a second URL, five non-absolute URLs, header @file / no colon / no name, a non-UTF-8 URL or header refused naming the field while a --data-binary $'\\xff\\xfe' body carries the bytes, and the Windows cmd shape refused as a stray ^. shapes (5): a Chrome bash command with continuations, $'...' body and --compressed asserting the whole request; Firefox; Safari; the literal toCurl outputs ui/src/flow.test.ts pins; the flow marked Imported, no response, no duration, HTTP/1.1, stamped at or after a captured instant. properties (5): the test-only Rust mirror of toCurl reproducing the TS file's pinned strings (the anchor that stops the mirror drifting silently); a_flow_rendered_by_to_curl_parses_back_to_itself over generated methods, URLs, headers with quotes/spaces/colons in their values, and bodies incl. one with quotes, backslashes and a newline - the generator excludes the two shapes where toCurl's own output is lossy as curl reads it (a blank header value, a GET with a body), stated beside it; never-panics over arbitrary text, arbitrary bytes, and a curl-shaped near-miss built from the parser's own vocabulary with values missing, doubled, attached, quoted, unterminated and malformed. src/api/routes.rs (3): ?format=har and ?format=curl each reach their parser and answer {imported:1} with the pasted row newest and status null; an unrecognised format is refused naming format and stores nothing; three refused commands (unsupported flag, second URL, non-UTF-8 URL) each answer 400 with the named token and leave the capture unchanged. The existing import helper delegates to the new query-carrying one so every W3 handler test runs unchanged against the dispatching handler."
  integration:
    count: 3
    covers: "tests/api.rs over the real axum router - this WU's bounded contract, the parser reached through the HTTP a client speaks. a_pasted_curl_command_lands_as_a_request_only_flow_marked_imported posts the Chrome-shaped fixture (continuations, -H lines, a $'...' body with an embedded quote, --compressed) to /flows/import?format=curl and reads the result back through every endpoint: the listing row (POST, the URL, source imported, status and duration null, request_body_state captured), the detail's request headers in order including the expanded Accept-Encoding, the request body bytes with their content-type, the response body endpoint answering 404 because there is no response leg, and the bus delivering a 'request' event carrying the new id - a pasted command lands exactly like a request the proxy saw. a_pasted_command_is_selected_by_provenance_beside_a_har_import imports a real export into a store holding a live flow, then pastes a command, and asserts ?source=imported returns all five with the pasted one newest, ?source=captured returns only the live row, and ?format=har still imports an empty document as {imported:0} - the HAR door unchanged under its own name. a_refused_curl_command_names_its_fault_and_leaves_the_store_untouched walks nine refusals (empty, wget, -F, -T, --data-urlencode, a second URL, -d @file, no URL, an unterminated quote from a truncated paste) each answering 400 with the expected token in the body and the listing byte-identical before and after, then an unknown ?format= refused naming format with the store still unchanged."
  e2e:
    waived: "The epic's own tests/e2e_export.rs step 6 owns this flow end to end - boot the engine, post a browser-shaped curl command to /flows/import?format=curl and then one carrying an unsupported flag, asserting the first appears as a request-only flow marked imported and the second is refused naming the flag - and the app half (a user pasting into the target W5 designed) rides the WebDriver suite after W6 builds it. A WU-level e2e would boot the same engine on ephemeral ports to assert what that step already asserts; the parser touches no socket and no file, so nothing about it is only observable through a running process. Same waiver reasoning W1-W4 recorded, and the epic gate re-walks it."
note: >-
  E-EXPORT.W7 delivers the inverse of the app's copy-as-curl: POST /flows/import?format=curl reads
  one pasted curl command as one request-only flow marked imported. Three things carry the WU.
  First, the accepted set is the one the epic plan settled at review (question 8) and it is the
  code's single lookup: Flag::lookup's arms ARE the plan's accepted and ignored tables, so a token
  in neither is refused by name - never guessed - because a silently dropped -F or -G yields a
  flow subtly unlike the request the user pasted, which is the one failure a paste target must
  not have. Second, the header-in-disguise rule: -u, -b and --compressed expand into the header
  each one IS, while curl's runtime defaults (Host, User-Agent, Accept, Content-Length, the
  form Content-Type it assumes for -d) are never invented - the flow records the command, not one
  tool's runtime, and this is also what makes toCurl -> parse an exact inverse rather than one
  that grows headers on every trip. Third, the round trip is proven as a property although toCurl
  is TypeScript: a test-only Rust mirror drives the proptest, and that mirror is anchored to the
  literal strings ui/src/flow.test.ts already pins, so it cannot drift from the TS without a test
  naming the drift. Writing the property found the inverse's boundary - a blank header value
  (toCurl writes -H 'Name: ', which curl reads as send-no-such-header) and a GET carrying a body
  (curl POSTs it) - and the parser was NOT bent to match: it agrees with curl, the generator
  excludes both shapes with the reason beside it, and DEC-32 records the boundary. The tokenizer
  is written in the crate over bytes rather than pulled in: the subset is small, every refusal
  needs its own wording, bash's $'...' (which Chrome emits for any body with a control character
  or a !) is outside what shell-words crates read, and bytes let a $'\xff' body byte survive while
  every non-body field is UTF-8-checked where it is read. The command travels as the raw request
  body, not JSON-wrapped, because a wrapper adds a quoting layer to an input whose whole
  difficulty is quoting. The refusal shape is W3's plain-text 400 - the shape W5's design run
  pinned as its one blocked-on before this parser existed and designed the paste target's Notice
  to render verbatim - so every reason is one lowercase sentence with the offending token quoted.
  One handler-level fact worth naming: a test expectation in the integration table was wrong on
  first writing - curl 'http://a/ -H 'A: 1' is an unterminated quote (a truncated paste), not a
  second URL - and the test was corrected to the parser's correct answer, not the parser to the
  test. Suite green: 218 lib unit, 35 tests/api.rs, 3 tests/ca.rs, 6 tests/e2e_capture.rs,
  4 tests/e2e_h2.rs, 0 failures; cargo fmt --check and clippy -D warnings clean across all targets.
